\f0\fs24 \cf2 At first glance, it seems like just an elegant bathtub, a tribute to Renaissance art\'85 but don't be fooled: this is one of the first hot water showers in the history of Milan. We\'92re at the end of the 19th century, electricity arrives in the city in 1883, and Casa Bagatti Valsecchi is one of the first private homes to use it. That\'92s why this bathtub, powered by both hot and cold running water, is a revolution. Look up carefully: that bronze rosette isn\'92t just decorative. It\'92s the showerhead! That\'92s right, a real shower, incredibly convenient and modern for the time. And it doesn\'92t end there: this house is a true technological gem. Electric wiring, gas heating... everything designed to bring the future into the walls of a home that seems to come from the past. But there\'92s also poetry, not just technology. This bathroom tells a symbolic story: the water flowing, the earth evoked by the sculpted amphibians, the fire in the lit fireplace, the air lightly flowing through the room. An environment where technology and nature shake hands. A place that speaks of well-being, harmony, and a vision of modernity that doesn\'92t forgo beauty.\
